---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Ref from '../components/Ref.astro';
import BarSet from '../components/BarSet.astro';
import StatGrid from '../components/StatGrid.astro';
import { ENGINES, ENGINES_CAVEAT, ECONOMY_RANKS, VALUE_PER_ACRE, RECIRCULATION, IMPORT_GAPS } from '../data/pages.js';
---

<Base
  title="Economy — Oklahoma Inc."
  description="A $213.5 billion economy, its engine room, where its land value concentrates, and what it buys but does not make."
  sources={['economy','tribal','land','local']}
  notes={[
    { title: 'On the sector figures', text: 'These mix BEA value-added (government, real estate, professional services) with total-activity and impact estimates (oil and gas, aerospace, tribal). They overlap and are not additive. They show relative heft, not a breakdown of GDP.' },
    { title: 'On value per acre', text: 'Value is 2024 gross assessed valuation from the Oklahoma Tax Commission divided by county land area. The table lists the extremes; the map covers all 77 counties.' },
    { title: 'On the import gaps', text: 'These state where demand exists alongside a local supply gap. No claim is made about why, or about who should fill them.' },
  ]}
  image="/economy.jpg"
  imageAlt="Oklahoma industry"
>
  <section class="panel panel--tight">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h1 class="mega">A $213.5 billion<br />machine.</h1>
      <p class="under">
        Step outside the capitol and the bigger income statement is the whole economy.
        It grew 1.5% in real terms in 2025, and its engine room has diversified well
        beyond the derrick.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">Oil still leads.<br />It no longer<br />stands alone.</h2>

      <div class="pad">
        <BarSet
          caption="Headline output and footprint by sector"
          rows={ENGINES}
          ref="economy"
          scale={60.3}
        />
      </div>
      <Ref to="on-the-sector-figures" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">Where it ranks</h2>
      <div><StatGrid stats={ECONOMY_RANKS} /></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">What that means</h2>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          The quiet story here is <strong>energy convergence</strong>. The same windy
          plains that made Oklahoma the sixth oil state now make it third in wind, and
          almost all of its electricity-generation growth since 2010 has been wind.
          Tinker Air Force Base seeded an aerospace cluster now worth $44 billion and
          206,000 jobs.
        </p>
        <p>
          Concentration cuts both ways. Oil and gas is still about 23% of all economic
          activity and the swing factor in state tax receipts. When crude rolls over,
          gross-production tax can fall half a billion dollars in a single year — which is
          exactly what happened in FY2024.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--tint">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">An acre is an acre<br />until you measure<br />what it earns.</h2>
      <p class="under">
        Every one of Oklahoma's 77 counties, measured by assessed property value divided
        by land area. The range runs from $57 an acre in Cimarron, out in the panhandle,
        to $24,831 an acre in Oklahoma County — a 436-fold spread.
      </p>

      <!-- The interactive choropleth of all 77 counties, carried over from
           the previous site. The table below it is a readable fallback and
           covers the extremes; the map covers every county. -->
      <figure class="dwmap pad">
        <iframe
          title="Property value per acre, by Oklahoma county"
          src="https://datawrapper.dwcdn.net/IKYax/2/"
          scrolling="no"
          frameborder="0"
          height="320"
          loading="lazy"
          data-external="1"
        ></iframe>
        <figcaption class="source">
          Hover or tap a county for its figure.<Ref to="land" label="the county map" />
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <Ref to="on-value-per-acre" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">The extremes</h2>
      <div class="tablewrap">
        <table class="vpa">
          <caption class="visually-hidden">Assessed value and value per acre, selected counties</caption>
          <thead>
            <tr><th scope="col">County</th><th scope="col" class="num">Assessed value</th><th scope="col" class="num">Value per acre</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {VALUE_PER_ACRE.map((r) => (
              <tr><th scope="row">{r[0]}</th><td class="num">{r[1]}</td><td class="num">{r[2]}</td></tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose pad">
        <p>
          Oklahoma and Tulsa counties together hold roughly <strong>38% of the entire
          state property tax base on about 3% of its land</strong>. Infrastructure —
          roads, pipes, utilities — is built and maintained across the whole map, while
          the value that funds it concentrates in a few points. Every mile of new
          infrastructure is a long-term maintenance liability for the full tax base.
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--deep">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">Not all spending<br />is equal.</h2>
      <p class="under">
        When a dollar is spent at a locally-owned business, a measurably larger share
        recirculates within the community before it leaves. This is a documented
        economic effect, not an argument.
      </p>

      <div class="pad">
        <BarSet
          caption="Estimated share of revenue that recirculates locally before leaving the state"
          rows={RECIRCULATION}
          ref="local"
          scale={100}
          deep={true}
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="panel panel--open">
    <div class="wrap">
      <h2 class="mega mega--sm">What Oklahoma buys<br />but does not make.</h2>

      <ul class="gaps pad">
        {IMPORT_GAPS.map((g) => (
          <li class="gap reveal">
            <h3 class="gap__title">{g[0]}</h3>
            <p class="gap__body">{g[1]}</p>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
      <Ref to="on-the-import-gaps" />
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>

<script>
  // Datawrapper posts its rendered height back; without this the frame is
  // fixed at its fallback height and clips on narrow screens.
  const sizeMaps = () => {
    window.addEventListener('message', (event) => {
      const data = event.data?.['datawrapper-height'];
      if (!data) return;
      document.querySelectorAll('iframe').forEach((frame) => {
        for (const key in data) {
          if (frame.contentWindow === event.source) {
            frame.style.height = data[key] + 'px';
          }
        }
      });
    });
  };
  sizeMaps();
  document.addEventListener('astro:page-load', sizeMaps);
</script>

<style>
  .dwmap { margin: 0; }
  .dwmap iframe {
    width: 100%;
    max-height: 60vh;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--surface);
  }
  .dwmap figcaption { margin-block-start: var(--s-3); }

  .tablewrap { overflow-x: auto; }

  .vpa {
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 32rem;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    font-size: var(--t-small);
  }
  .vpa :is(th, td) {
    text-align: left;
    padding: var(--s-3) var(--s-4) var(--s-3) 0;
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .vpa thead th {
    font-size: var(--t-micro);
    letter-spacing: var(--track-wide);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-3);
    font-weight: 600;
  }
  .vpa tbody th { font-weight: 500; }
  .vpa .num {
    text-align: right;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
    font-family: var(--font-display);
    font-weight: 600;
  }

  .gaps {
    list-style: none;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(20rem, 100%), 1fr));
    gap: 1px;
    background: var(--rule);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  .gap { background: var(--surface); padding: var(--s-5); }
  .gap__title { font-size: var(--t-body); font-weight: 600; margin-block-end: var(--s-2); }
  .gap__body { font-size: var(--t-small); color: var(--ink-2); }

  .visually-hidden {
    position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px;
    padding: 0; margin: -1px; overflow: hidden;
    clip-path: inset(50%); white-space: nowrap;
  }
</style>
